  "query_preview": "..\n\nA moist airmass in place, as the 12z DTX RAOB checked in with a PW \nvalue of 1.99 inches. Thick cloud cover and numerous to widespread \nshowers tracking through southeast Michigan have limited \ninstability, and thus thunderstorm development has been hard to come \nby. Do not expect any severe storms as MLcape remains under 1000 \nJ/kg through the remainder of the day, and SPC Day 1 has adjusted \nthe marginal risk south. \n\nShowers end by early this evening as the 850-700 MB Theta-E ridge is \non track to drop south of the border this evening, with much drier \nair advecting in through Friday morning, when PW Values bottom out \naround 0.5 inch. With this drier air mass and a 1018 MB surface high \nover southeast Michigan, efficient radiational cooling areas will \nlikely see min temps aoa 50 degrees, warming into the upper 70s \nduring the day under mostly sunny skies. \n\nThe large anti-cyclone in place on Friday will be slow to exit east, \nand associated 850-700 MB Theta-E trough looks to hold through \nSaturday morning before a weak cold front and moisture axis \napproaches from the western Great Lakes. Warm mid levels (up to 9 C \nat 700 MB) and weak low level convergence certainly could support a \nmainly dry frontal passage Saturday evening/night, or even a washing \nout boundary that promotes some fog development. Outgoing NBM pops \nare under 15 percent Saturday through Sunday.",
